{-
   Where the stream goes on JavaScript - Design-Test.md §5.2, and §11.2's F4 note.

   There is one output here and it is `console`, so the separation the native half buys with
   descriptor 3 does not exist on this target. That is the same asymmetry the design states outright
   for the harness: the JavaScript target gets the runner, orchestration happens in a native parent
   that spawns `node`, and a fake second stream would be a lie about which of the two is which.

   `hostLog` appends a newline of its own, so the trailing one every caller writes is trimmed rather
   than doubled - the line *is* the unit here, and a blank line between every two of them would make
   the stream harder to read for no gain.
-}

import Native

-- There is one output on this target, so the stream never has a channel of its own and the runner
-- always reports for a human - see the native half, where this is a real question.
pub fn reportIsPrivate() -> Bool = False

-- And the other side of that: `print` and this are both `console.log`, so a run that reports for a
-- human and does not mute the stream writes the two reports of §5.5 into one another. Natively they
-- are two descriptors and the answer is no.
pub fn reportSharesOutput() -> Bool = True

pub fn reportLine(text: String) -> {}:
    if reportMuted() then return

    let count = length(text)
    if count == (0 :: Size) then return

    -- The one code unit this has to look at. Slicing a `String` is not in the portable surface, so
    -- the trim is a rebuild - which on this target is a host string concatenation and is what every
    -- other string operation here already is.
    if text.unit(count - 1) != 10:
        hostLog(text)
        return

    let &line = String.ofCapacity(count - 1)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count - 1:
        line.pushUnit(text.unit(i))
        i = i + 1

    hostLog(line)
